/-!
# The base category of a deformation problem

The base category `𝒞_Λ` of a deformation problem is the category of Artinian local
`Λ`-algebras carrying a chosen identification of their residue field with a fixed field `k`,
and local `Λ`-algebra maps compatible with those identifications. This file constructs that
category and records the elementary facts about its morphisms: which surjections are small
extensions, that every surjection factors into small extensions, and when a small extension
admits a section.

## Main definitions

* `ArtinianLocalAlgCat` is the category of Artinian local `Λ`-algebras with
  residue field identified with `k`.
* `ArtinianLocalAlgCat.Hom` is its type of compatible local algebra maps.
* `IsClassical` says that `Λ` is Noetherian complete local and that its residue
  field is identified with `k` as a `Λ`-algebra.
* `InfinitesimalLifting.IsSmallExtension` is the predicate for small extensions.
* `IsEssentialSurjection` is the predicate that a morphism in `𝒞_Λ` is an
  essential surjection.
* `relativeCotangentSpace` is the relative cotangent space of a local ring homomorphism.
* `residueImageField` is the subfield of `k` generated by the image
  of `Λ`.
* `ArtinianLocalAlgCat.relativeCotangentSpaceOverBase` is the quotient
  `m_A / (m_Λ A + m_A²)`, where `m_Λ = ker(Λ → k)`.
* `ArtinianLocalAlgCat.relativeCotangentSpaceMap` is its functorial map.

## Main results

* `ArtinianLocalAlgCat.residue_comm_iff` identifies compatibility on
  representatives with commutativity of the residue-field triangle.
* `surjective_factors_into_smallExtensions` gives the finite factorization.
* `not_isEssentialSurjection_iff_exists_section`
  characterizes nonessential small extensions by the existence of a section.
* `residueImageField_eq_range` identifies this subfield with the
  image of the finite map.
* `finrank_residueImageField_eq_quotient_ker` is the equality of
  degrees `[k : k'] = [k : Λ / ker(Λ → k)]`.
* `residueImageField_finite` says that this degree is finite.
* `finrank_residueImageField_mul_length_eq_length` compares length over
  a local algebra with length over its base ring.
* `length_eq_length_of_isClassical` gives the comparison in the classical case.
* `ArtinianLocalAlgCat.surjective_iff_cotangentSpaceMap_surjective` detects
  surjectivity on `m / m²`.
* `cotangentSpaceMap_surjective_iff_relativeCotangentSpaceMap_surjective` compares the
  absolute and relative criteria.

## Implementation notes

### Artinian local algebras with fixed residue field

Compatibility of a morphism with the residue-field identifications is recorded after
precomposition with the surjective residue map. This is equivalent to commutativity of the
triangle on residue fields by `ArtinianLocalAlgCat.residue_comm_iff`, and makes identities
and composition definitionally direct. Morphisms expose their locality both for their
underlying algebra homomorphism and for its underlying ring homomorphism.
The category is defined without the Noetherian and finiteness assumptions on `Λ → k`, since
neither is needed to form it. The classical case uses the existing predicate
`IsNoetherianCompleteLocalRing` for completeness in the maximal-ideal-adic topology.

### Small extensions

We adopt `InfinitesimalLifting.IsSmallExtension`, already used for Stacks Project Tag 02HS,
rather than duplicate it for morphisms in `𝒞_Λ`. Its length-one condition says that the kernel
is a simple module. Over the local Artinian source of a morphism in `𝒞_Λ`, this is equivalent to
the source formulation: a nonzero principal ideal annihilated by the maximal ideal. Thus the
adopted predicate applies directly after viewing the underlying ring homomorphism as a morphism
of bundled commutative rings.

### Essential surjections of Artinian local algebras

The definition is phrased directly in terms of surjectivity of the underlying algebra
homomorphisms. It is an abbreviation, so clients can use the conjunction and the standard
API for `Function.Surjective` directly.

### Factoring surjections into small extensions

The finite factorization is represented by `CategoryTheory.ComposableArrows`. Its source is
identified with the source of the given morphism, while its target is isomorphic to the target
of the given morphism; the latter isomorphism is the quotient identification in the source
proof. These identifications are included in the displayed composite. Each consecutive
underlying ring map is required to be a small extension. The terminal isomorphism is
essential even for a surjective isomorphism that is not the identity.

### Sections of small extensions

The small-extension condition is imposed on the underlying morphism of bundled commutative
rings. The section and its identity law remain internal to the category `𝒞_Λ`, so they retain
compatibility with the fixed residue-field identifications.

### Relative cotangent spaces of local rings

The quotient is written as a quotient of the `S`-module `maximalIdeal S`.  The denominator
is pulled back along its inclusion into `S`; localness ensures that the extended maximal ideal
lies in `maximalIdeal S`.  The square of the maximal ideal annihilates this quotient, providing
its canonical module structure over `ResidueField S`.

### The residue image field of a finite map to a field

The image is initially only a subring in Lean.  We define `k'` as the subfield
it generates and record that finiteness of the original ring map makes its
underlying set exactly the image.  The algebra structure over the quotient in
the degree formula is induced by the canonical quotient map to `k`.

### Length over a local algebra and its base ring

The residue field `k'` is represented by `residueImageField Λ k`.  The
chosen `Λ`-algebra equivalence from the residue field of `A` to `k` records the
phrase “with residue field `k`”.  The two assertions in the source are stated
separately so that the classical equality can be used directly.

### Surjectivity of maps of Artinian local algebras

The ordinary cotangent spaces and their maps use `Ideal.Cotangent` and
`Ideal.mapCotangent`. In this chapter `m_Λ` means the kernel of the fixed map `Λ → k`,
not the maximal ideal of a local ring.  The relative cotangent space is therefore written
directly as a quotient of `m_A`; this also covers the nonlocal bases allowed in `𝒞_Λ`.

